The table of contents is generated automatically based on the section headings in the document. By default, `tocstyle` uses a simple two-level table of contents with chapter headings at the top level and section headings at the second level. However, you can customize the appearance of the table of contents using various options provided by the `tocstyle` package.

Note that the table of contents is generated at the beginning of the document by default. If you want to place it at the end of the document, you can use the `\addtocontents` command to add a custom location for the table of contents. For example, you can use `\addtocontents{toc}{lof}{chapter}` to place the table of contents after the list of figures and before the list of tables.

